>
  <p class="answer"><strong>Short answer:</strong> Vipassana is Buddhist insight meditation: you train calm, non-judging awareness of the breath, body sensations, and thoughts. In Chiang Mai you can learn it three main ways. You can join a traditional temple course lasting a week or more, a 10-day silent course, or a shorter guided class in English. 347 Awakening teaches the guided way at the Silver Temple (Wat Sri Suphan), starting with a 3-hour session for complete beginners.</p>

  <h2>What is Vipassana meditation?</h2>
  <p>Vipassana (pronounced <em>vi-pah-sa-na</em>) comes from Pali, the language of the early Buddhist texts, and means "insight" or "seeing clearly". It is the core meditation of Theravada Buddhism, the tradition of Thailand, Myanmar and Sri Lanka.</p>
  <p>Instead of trying to empty the mind or visualise something, you <strong>observe what is actually happening</strong>: the breath moving in and out, sensations in the body, thoughts and emotions arising and passing. Over time, this clear observation shows you that feelings and thoughts are temporary. They stop controlling you so easily, and that is where the calm and clarity come from.</p>

  <h2>How Vipassana is taught</h2>
  <p>Teachers differ, but most Vipassana instruction combines a few core practices:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sitting meditation:</strong> resting attention on the breath, often the rise and fall of the abdomen or the sensation at the nostrils.</li>
    <li><strong>Walking meditation:</strong> slow, deliberate steps, noticing lifting, moving and placing each foot. It's very helpful if sitting still is hard for you.</li>
    <li><strong>Noting or body scanning:</strong> gently labelling experiences ("thinking", "hearing") or moving attention systematically through the body.</li>
    <li><strong>Mindfulness in daily life:</strong> bringing the same awareness to eating, walking and conversation, so the practice doesn't stay on the cushion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why learn Vipassana in Chiang Mai?</h2>
  <p>Chiang Mai has hundreds of temples and a long tradition of welcoming foreign meditators. Practicing in a living temple puts you in an atmosphere of quiet and devotion that is hard to find anywhere else. The city is also easy to stay in: affordable guesthouses, vegetarian food, and a cooler climate than Bangkok.</p>

